WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.536 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:03.536 --> 00:00:08.874
-Hey, everybody, welcome to our
first corrections of December.

00:00:08.874 --> 00:00:11.777
Some people tried to correct me

00:00:11.777 --> 00:00:15.047
for saying
"'chomping' at the bit."

00:00:15.047 --> 00:00:18.951
They said the correct use was
"'champing' at the bit."

00:00:18.951 --> 00:00:21.854
You know, to mean
someone who is anxious,

00:00:21.854 --> 00:00:23.489
who wants to get to it.

00:00:23.489 --> 00:00:26.125
I checked.
It was originally "champing."

00:00:26.125 --> 00:00:28.661
Both are acceptable.

00:00:28.661 --> 00:00:30.129
I don't like using "champ."

00:00:30.129 --> 00:00:31.998
I thought "champing" meant

00:00:31.998 --> 00:00:34.233
when President Biden
was so anxious,

00:00:34.233 --> 00:00:36.769
he ordered his dog to bite you.

00:00:36.769 --> 00:00:39.272
[ Light laughter ]

00:00:40.807 --> 00:00:43.009
So, we did
a "Jokes Seth Can't Tell,"

00:00:43.009 --> 00:00:46.145
where our own Jenny Hagel
spoke some Spanish.

00:00:46.145 --> 00:00:50.516
And one viewer said
that she conjugated

00:00:50.516 --> 00:00:55.855
the Spanish verbs "murare"
and "decir" incorrectly.

00:00:55.855 --> 00:00:58.758
Alright, if that's true,

00:00:58.758 --> 00:01:00.827
no está bien.

00:01:00.827 --> 00:01:03.595
[ Laughter ]

00:01:03.595 --> 00:01:06.065
But if it's not true,

00:01:06.065 --> 00:01:11.704
and you are incorrectly
telling Jenny she's wrong,

00:01:11.704 --> 00:01:13.973
I would not want to be you.

00:01:13.973 --> 00:01:15.942
[ Laughter ]

00:01:15.942 --> 00:01:19.145
Because Jen-- Well, it's like
what you always say about Jenny.

00:01:19.145 --> 00:01:21.381
Okay, you don't want
to say it now?

00:01:21.381 --> 00:01:23.683
[ Light laughter ]

00:01:23.683 --> 00:01:26.019
Shoemaker, you don't want to say
what you always say?

00:01:26.019 --> 00:01:27.686
[ Light laughter ]

00:01:27.686 --> 00:01:29.355
Say it all the time
behind closed doors.

00:01:29.355 --> 00:01:30.957
[ Light laughter ]

00:01:30.957 --> 00:01:33.426
She takes conjugation
very serious.

00:01:33.426 --> 00:01:36.596
[ Laughter ]

00:01:38.198 --> 00:01:41.867
You both summon Beetlejuice

00:01:41.867 --> 00:01:43.536
by saying his name three times,

00:01:43.536 --> 00:01:45.138
and you banish him, three times.

00:01:45.138 --> 00:01:46.973
I got -- People have said
I got it wrong.

00:01:46.973 --> 00:01:49.208
Both are fine. And shame on you.

00:01:49.208 --> 00:01:50.877
I also -- People said,

00:01:50.877 --> 00:01:52.945
"I thought, when you were
calling Andy Samberg

00:01:52.945 --> 00:01:55.114
you were calling
Michael Keaton."

00:01:55.114 --> 00:01:56.983
I got his number.

00:01:56.983 --> 00:01:58.384
[ Light laughter ]

00:01:58.384 --> 00:02:01.154
Just not gonna call him
in the middle of corrections.

00:02:04.857 --> 00:02:08.761
Someone wrote, this was a --
It felt very earnest.

00:02:08.761 --> 00:02:12.031
They said, "I would love

00:02:12.031 --> 00:02:15.801
if you could make corrections
more self-contained

00:02:15.801 --> 00:02:19.539
so that I don't have to watch
the rest of the show

00:02:19.539 --> 00:02:21.708
to get the jokes."

00:02:21.708 --> 00:02:25.144
[ Laughter ]

00:02:25.144 --> 00:02:28.080
It's like someone saying,

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:31.284
"Could the letters to the editor
just be about the editor?"

00:02:31.284 --> 00:02:33.586
[ Laughter ]

00:02:33.586 --> 00:02:35.955
[ Clears throat ]

00:02:35.955 --> 00:02:38.825
I said "crew chi--"
I said "crew team."

00:02:38.825 --> 00:02:41.161
We were talking about coxswains.

00:02:43.028 --> 00:02:45.031
We said "crew team."

00:02:45.031 --> 00:02:47.200
It's either
"rowing team" or "crew."

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:49.435
It is redundant
to say "crew team."

00:02:49.435 --> 00:02:52.472
Be like saying
"insufferable jackals."

00:02:52.472 --> 00:02:55.341
[ Light laughter ]

00:02:55.341 --> 00:02:59.045
Some British people
have been complaining

00:02:59.045 --> 00:03:02.214
that I call out
a lot of the contradictions

00:03:02.214 --> 00:03:04.417
in British pronunciation

00:03:04.417 --> 00:03:06.518
while ignoring
that American English

00:03:06.518 --> 00:03:09.188
also has
the same contradictions.

00:03:09.188 --> 00:03:11.323
Someone from England said,

00:03:11.323 --> 00:03:12.926
"Alright, how about this?

00:03:12.926 --> 00:03:16.529
How come you call
one of your states Kansas

00:03:16.529 --> 00:03:19.164
and another one Ar-kansas?"

00:03:19.164 --> 00:03:21.234
I don't get what her point was.

00:03:21.234 --> 00:03:24.503
[ Light laughter ]

00:03:24.503 --> 00:03:28.474
"It's not 'impart upon.'
It's 'impart to.'"

00:03:28.474 --> 00:03:33.613
"It's 'suffice to say,'
not 'suffice it to say.'"

00:03:33.613 --> 00:03:36.782
Again, I've gone through this
before.

00:03:36.782 --> 00:03:40.119
I hate when you just burn me
on a flub.

00:03:40.119 --> 00:03:42.221
I did, in
the Thanksgiving show --

00:03:42.221 --> 00:03:44.657
or the Thanksgiving corrections,
I guess it was --

00:03:44.657 --> 00:03:48.293
pronounce "pilgrim," "pigrim."
[ Chuckles ]

00:03:48.293 --> 00:03:50.963
Obviously, it's not "pigrim."
"Pigrim" isn't a word.

00:03:50.963 --> 00:03:52.831
"Pilgram" is a word.

00:03:52.831 --> 00:03:55.034
I looked this up,
'cause I thought so.

00:03:55.034 --> 00:03:58.504
A Pilgram is a pilgrim's name

00:03:58.504 --> 00:04:00.707
that uses all seven letters.

00:04:00.707 --> 00:04:02.976
[ Laughter ]

00:04:10.182 --> 00:04:13.319
Uh, I talked about
how excited I am

00:04:13.319 --> 00:04:14.653
to get back on the subway,

00:04:14.653 --> 00:04:16.155
and that when
I get on the subway,

00:04:16.155 --> 00:04:17.556
I'm gonna high-five a rat.

00:04:17.556 --> 00:04:19.459
Can't high-five a rat.

00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:21.460
Rats only have four fingers.

00:04:21.460 --> 00:04:25.965
So, if you ever want to
celebrate with a rat,

00:04:25.965 --> 00:04:28.034
say "up top."

00:04:28.034 --> 00:04:31.003
[ Light laughter ]

00:04:31.003 --> 00:04:34.673
"I heard 'just salad.'
Threw out some old lettuce.

00:04:34.673 --> 00:04:36.609
Up top."

00:04:36.609 --> 00:04:38.911
[ Light laughter ]

00:04:38.911 --> 00:04:40.712
Talked about Donald Trump

00:04:40.712 --> 00:04:43.282
meeting the green M&amp;M backstage

00:04:43.282 --> 00:04:45.918
at a Bon Jovi concert

00:04:45.918 --> 00:04:48.321
in 1986.

00:04:48.321 --> 00:04:52.592
The sexy, green M&amp;M was not
introduced until 1995...

00:04:54.593 --> 00:04:58.297
...because it wasn't
until 1995...

00:04:58.297 --> 00:05:00.733
I mean, this is interesting

00:05:00.733 --> 00:05:03.635
because it just speaks
to how much norms change

00:05:03.635 --> 00:05:05.738
over time.

00:05:05.738 --> 00:05:10.143
Before 1995, it was considered
weird to want to [Bleep] candy.

00:05:10.143 --> 00:05:13.212
[ Laughter ]

00:05:18.885 --> 00:05:20.386
[ Chuckles ]

00:05:20.386 --> 00:05:22.588
It's gonna be -- I think it's --
It feels like it's gonna be

00:05:22.588 --> 00:05:24.423
a little bit
of a shorter corrections,

00:05:24.423 --> 00:05:27.493
which I think is both because we
had a really good week,

00:05:27.493 --> 00:05:30.463
and also, next week is gonna be

00:05:30.463 --> 00:05:32.131
the last corrections
of the year.

00:05:32.131 --> 00:05:36.435
And what I'd like to do
is make it special,

00:05:36.435 --> 00:05:39.438
like, you know,
a New Year's special.

00:05:39.438 --> 00:05:42.074
We did -- This show did
a New Year's Eve special.

00:05:42.074 --> 00:05:44.210
Do you remember our prime-time
New Year's Eve special?

00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:46.612
I think, 2016.

00:05:46.612 --> 00:05:51.417
And, um, I don't remember
the exact ratings.

00:05:51.417 --> 00:05:54.387
I just know that Nielsen
sent us a letter,

00:05:54.387 --> 00:05:56.522
which was really nice --
like, registered mail

00:05:56.522 --> 00:05:58.624
that said it "ate [Bleep]."

00:05:58.624 --> 00:06:01.094
[ Laughter ]

00:06:01.094 --> 00:06:02.962
We haven't been asked to do

00:06:02.962 --> 00:06:04.864
another prime-time
New Year's Eve special.

00:06:04.864 --> 00:06:05.965
But I'd like to do

00:06:05.965 --> 00:06:07.266
a New Year's Eve
correction special,

00:06:07.266 --> 00:06:08.868
where I'd like to count down --

00:06:08.868 --> 00:06:10.469
I don't want to do any work.

00:06:10.469 --> 00:06:14.240
I want to count down the best
corrections of the year.

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:16.676
So, again, I'd like
to outsource this to you,

00:06:16.676 --> 00:06:20.012
in the comments
of this corrections

00:06:20.012 --> 00:06:21.914
that you're watching now,
if you have

00:06:21.914 --> 00:06:24.150
a favorite correction
from this year,

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:27.587
just write it
with the hashtag #bestof.

00:06:27.587 --> 00:06:30.089
Think that's simple enough.

00:06:30.089 --> 00:06:32.391
And then, next week,

00:06:32.391 --> 00:06:34.727
I think, you know, assuming

00:06:34.727 --> 00:06:36.963
how many good ones we've had,

00:06:36.963 --> 00:06:39.799
I'd guess we're looking at,
like, a two-hour corrections.

00:06:39.799 --> 00:06:42.101
[ Laughter ]

00:06:42.101 --> 00:06:44.503
You know, that's just for
my crew to plan accordingly.

00:06:44.503 --> 00:06:47.172
[ Laughter ]

00:06:47.172 --> 00:06:49.508
This one, um --

00:06:49.508 --> 00:06:52.545
This one is the one
I feel worst about this week.

00:06:52.545 --> 00:06:55.314
So, in the Thanksgiving show,

00:06:55.314 --> 00:06:57.450
I had my parents,
my brother here.

00:06:57.450 --> 00:06:59.618
And we made an announcement --
a very special announcement

00:06:59.618 --> 00:07:03.088
about the fact that my wife
gave birth on September 15th

00:07:03.088 --> 00:07:04.590
and we have a baby girl
in our family.

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:06.459
And we talked a lot
about the fact

00:07:06.459 --> 00:07:10.796
that we named our daughter
Adelaide,

00:07:10.796 --> 00:07:12.965
which was my grandmother's name.

00:07:12.965 --> 00:07:16.435
And we used a lot of the time
on the Thanksgiving show

00:07:16.435 --> 00:07:20.839
to share our memories
of my grandmother Adelaide

00:07:20.839 --> 00:07:23.175
and how important it was to us

00:07:23.175 --> 00:07:27.112
that we had named her
after this incredible woman.

00:07:27.112 --> 00:07:30.850
I failed to mention
that her middle name is Ruth,

00:07:30.850 --> 00:07:34.253
which is the name of
my wife's grandmother.

00:07:34.253 --> 00:07:37.623
And this is something I caught
on my own.

00:07:37.623 --> 00:07:41.226
This wasn't a situation
where, on Friday,

00:07:41.226 --> 00:07:44.229
I was sitting with both families
watching it,

00:07:44.229 --> 00:07:47.300
and halfway through it,
my wife turned to me

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:50.402
and whispered,
"Did you not mention Ruth?"

00:07:50.402 --> 00:07:52.638
and I said, "Oh, no."

00:07:52.638 --> 00:07:54.940
[ Laughter ]

00:07:54.940 --> 00:07:57.008
That didn't happen.

00:07:57.008 --> 00:07:59.712
I caught it on my own.

00:07:59.712 --> 00:08:02.748
As soon as the show was over,
I said to Shoemaker,

00:08:02.748 --> 00:08:04.850
"Oh, man, I screwed up."

00:08:04.850 --> 00:08:08.321
I forgot to mention
that her middle name is Ruth.

00:08:08.321 --> 00:08:10.522
We named her after two
incredible women,

00:08:10.522 --> 00:08:12.458
not just Adelaide."

00:08:12.458 --> 00:08:14.894
You remember me saying that,
Shoemaker.

00:08:14.894 --> 00:08:17.096
[ Laughter ]

00:08:17.096 --> 00:08:19.332
Don't [Bleep] me, Shoemaker.

00:08:19.332 --> 00:08:22.502
[ Laughter ]

00:08:25.003 --> 00:08:29.442
And I think it's more meaningful
that I caught it on my own.

00:08:29.442 --> 00:08:32.512
[ Laughter ]

00:08:34.247 --> 00:08:36.781
I don't even know why,
in the context --

00:08:36.781 --> 00:08:38.383
Oh, here's what happened.

00:08:38.383 --> 00:08:40.152
So, the Thanksgiving show,

00:08:40.152 --> 00:08:41.653
you know, it's always
my parents,

00:08:41.653 --> 00:08:43.522
and we always have -- you know,
we don't have "A Closer Look"

00:08:43.522 --> 00:08:45.824
on the Thanksgiving show,
and we've done over the years --

00:08:45.824 --> 00:08:49.094
you know, back in my day,
we've done "Clear the Air,"

00:08:49.094 --> 00:08:52.365
probably moved out
a few "Teen Slangs," "Ya Burnt."

00:08:52.365 --> 00:08:54.901
And so this year, we did
a surprise inspection,

00:08:54.901 --> 00:08:59.705
which is this new segment
where we take

00:08:59.705 --> 00:09:01.841
the most embarrassing jokes
that our writers have submitted,

00:09:01.841 --> 00:09:04.443
and without them knowing,
read them on air.

00:09:04.443 --> 00:09:08.781
And it's super fun.

00:09:08.781 --> 00:09:10.282
But, you know,
the Thanksgiving show

00:09:10.282 --> 00:09:11.817
is our highest-rated show
of the year

00:09:11.817 --> 00:09:13.586
because it's on
after NFL football

00:09:13.586 --> 00:09:16.455
and/or because my parents
are on, if you ask them.

00:09:16.455 --> 00:09:18.590
And highest-rated show
of the year.

00:09:18.590 --> 00:09:20.593
And you know, there were some
that were so --

00:09:20.593 --> 00:09:22.761
'cause a lot of them
are really groaners --

00:09:22.761 --> 00:09:24.630
we rarely do this --

00:09:24.630 --> 00:09:27.199
we edited out
like three of the jokes.

00:09:27.199 --> 00:09:29.634
Mostly Scollins jokes.

00:09:29.634 --> 00:09:31.703
And I mention that on air

00:09:31.703 --> 00:09:33.806
that Scollins writes jokes

00:09:33.806 --> 00:09:36.108
that are inappropriate
for Thanksgiving.

00:09:36.108 --> 00:09:38.377
And then someone --
one of our jackals --

00:09:38.377 --> 00:09:41.213
said, "The problem is, he
doesn't celebrate Thanksgiving.

00:09:41.213 --> 00:09:43.849
Scollins celebrates
Tanks-giving."

00:09:43.849 --> 00:09:46.118
[ Laughter ]

00:09:46.118 --> 00:09:48.521
Referencing the one time
that Mike Scollins

00:09:48.521 --> 00:09:51.958
wore a tank top, uh,
to the show.

00:09:51.958 --> 00:09:54.459
And...

00:09:54.459 --> 00:09:57.530
we've never let him
hear the end of it.

00:09:59.398 --> 00:10:01.267
And, uh...

00:10:01.267 --> 00:10:05.638
[Chuckling]

00:10:05.638 --> 00:10:08.040
I was thinking about it

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:10.142
'cause a lot of people said,
"Hey, that's really unfair.

00:10:10.142 --> 00:10:12.644
Like, what about us jackals?
We want to see all the jokes."

00:10:12.644 --> 00:10:15.715
And so we're gonna tell you --

00:10:15.715 --> 00:10:17.483
there was a Michael Jackson joke

00:10:17.483 --> 00:10:19.852
that we just didn't think was
appropriate for Thanksgiving.

00:10:19.852 --> 00:10:22.788
But before we do it,
I'd like to read this.

00:10:30.528 --> 00:10:32.931
According to a new study,

00:10:32.931 --> 00:10:35.701
children can tell
how people are feeling.

00:10:35.701 --> 00:10:38.837
Remember, this was for
the Thanksgiving show.

00:10:38.837 --> 00:10:41.039
[ Light laughter ]

00:10:41.039 --> 00:10:43.175
People are with their families.

00:10:43.175 --> 00:10:45.177
Been a long night.

00:10:45.177 --> 00:10:48.514
"Hey, look, watch Seth Meyers
and his family.

00:10:48.514 --> 00:10:50.148
I hear he might have
an announcement

00:10:50.148 --> 00:10:52.751
about a -- a new child --

00:10:52.751 --> 00:10:55.688
Adelaide Meyers.

00:10:57.823 --> 00:10:59.558
No middle name."

00:10:59.558 --> 00:11:02.627
[ Laughter ]

00:11:02.627 --> 00:11:04.697
According to a new study,

00:11:04.697 --> 00:11:06.699
children can tell
how people are feeling,

00:11:06.699 --> 00:11:08.333
even if they're wearing
a mask.

00:11:08.333 --> 00:11:10.269
For example, horny.

00:11:10.269 --> 00:11:13.506
[ Light laughter ]

00:11:15.206 --> 00:11:17.709
And that wraps it up.

00:11:17.709 --> 00:11:21.680
We will see you --
You will see me

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:24.917
next week for our
New Year's spectacular.

00:11:24.917 --> 00:11:26.419
[ Laughter ]

